<p>Dear Mr. Kozin,</p>
<p>As promised in my email of October 27, here are the details of the UAE f=
iling agent I propose for the two PCT applications.</p>
<p><b>Proposed agent.</b> Abu-Ghazaleh Intellectual Property (AGIP), one of=
 the largest intellectual property firms in the Arab region, with an office=
 in Dubai. AGIP has confirmed in writing that it has patent agents register=
ed with the UAE Ministry of Economy
 who are entitled to act before the International Bureau for a UAE applican=
t under PCT Rule 83.1bis. AGIP will send me the name and registration numbe=
r of the agent who will be named in the applications, and I will forward th=
em to you as soon as I receive them.</p>
<p><b>Role.</b> As described in my October 27 email: AGIP signs and submits=
 each application in ePCT exactly as I instruct, on the agreed filing date;=
 pays the WIPO fees; forwards all notices to me; gives me direct access to =
both applications in ePCT; and files
 any correction I prepare. I remain responsible for preparing and checking =
the applications and for instructing and supervising AGIP.</p>
<p><b>AGIP's fees.</b> USD 750 per application plus 5% UAE VAT, that is USD=
 787.50 per application, fixed. This includes the filing and forwarding all=
 routine notices through the international search report and written opinio=
n. Only if a correction or reply
 must be filed would AGIP charge a further USD 750 plus VAT, and only with =
your prior written approval. This replaces the estimate of USD 1,000=EF=BF=
=BD2,000 per application in my earlier proposal.</p>
<p><b>Official fees and invoicing.</b> AGIP will invoice PBK directly with =
a proforma invoice covering its fee, VAT and the WIPO fees (about CHF 2,819=
 per application with the Australian Office as searching authority). The in=
voice is paid before each filing
 date; AGIP pays WIPO from its own WIPO account and settles any exchange-ra=
te difference in a final statement, with WIPO's payment confirmation. AGIP =
issues the proforma once it receives the draft application, so that it can =
count the pages for the WIPO fees.</p>
<p><b>No power of attorney.</b> Because AGIP is named as agent in the reque=
st form, no power of attorney is needed for the international filings. The =
documents for your signature are therefore: acceptance of the engagement; t=
he Inventor's Declaration described
 in my October 27 email; PBK's USPTO power of attorney to me (form PTO/AIA/=
82), to download the as-filed provisional texts and arrange the priority do=
cuments; and AGIP's confidentiality agreement with PBK.</p>
<p><b>Confidentiality.</b> AGIP has confirmed it will keep PBK's identity a=
nd the application materials confidential, and it asks PBK to sign its stan=
dard non-disclosure agreement before receiving any materials. Before PBK si=
gns, I recommend three changes,
 which I will prepare for your review: completing PBK's details as the disc=
losing party; filling in the Annex, which is currently blank, because the a=
greement protects only the information listed there; and narrowing one excl=
usion that would release information
 obtained by any third party. The agreement is governed by Jordanian law, w=
hich I consider acceptable for this purpose.</p>
<p><b>Your approval.</b> To proceed with AGIP, I need your written approval=
 to (1) give AGIP the name of PBK for its conflict check, and (2) appoint A=
GIP as the filing agent, subject to your review of its confidentiality agre=
ement. I will not share any information
 about PBK or the inventions with AGIP before you approve.</p>
<p><b>Timing.</b> The schedule in my October 27 email assumed acceptance by=
 October 30. Each further day moves the drafting and review dates by a day.=
 The Group 2 statutory deadline of December 11 leaves limited room, so I wo=
uld be grateful for your decision
 this week.</p>
<p>Kind regards,</p>
<p>Michael L. Bartholomew<br>
Bartholomew Law PC<br>
Registered U.S. Patent Attorney, USPTO Reg. No. 60,225<br>
Highland, UT 84003 =EF=BF=BD Office 385.230.5310</p>
